sections.licensing_pathway:
**Who licenses you.** DCH HFRD issues the permit. Its office is at 2 Martin Luther King Jr. Drive SE, East Tower, Atlanta, GA 30334 (Monday–Friday, 8 a.m.–5 p.m. ET) [S8]; the DCH main line is 404-657-5700. A direct HFRD phone number was not verified. Questions on regulations, surveys, permits and administrator contact updates go to pchprogram.hfrd@dch.ga.gov [S4]. Application and waiver questions go to hfrd.applicationswaivers@dch.ga.gov, and payments to hfrd.payments@dch.ga.gov. GCHEXS background check support is at 404-463-7154 or gchexs.user@dch.ga.gov [S4]. ALC applications are filed through DCH's online GAHLES portal at https://gahles.dch.georgia.gov/ [S4]. The DCH checklist documented here is the **ALC** packet (updated 07/13/26). A PCH-specific application packet was not retrieved, so confirm the PCH document list with HFRD before you file.

**Step-by-step pathway**
1. **Form the entity.** When a home is owned by an LLC, the governing body of the LLC authorizes the license application and the LLC is the licensee [S18]. A Georgia LLC costs about $110 to form, and annual registration is about $60 all-in, though a second secondary source lists a $50 base fee [S42]. Neither figure comes from the Secretary of State, so verify both. The same secondary sources say Georgia imposes no franchise tax on LLCs [S50].
2. **Clear local zoning and siting.** The rule requires the applicant to address local zoning and location requirements with the responsible local officials [S1]. Zoning and local occupancy fees were not verified. A local occupational tax certificate is commonly required, reported at $10 to $1,000+ depending on the locality [S52].
3. **For an ALC, obtain the OHP Letter of Determination.** The ALC checklist lists a Letter of Determination approved by the DCH Office of Health Planning as a required attachment [S4]. Its fee and timeline were not verified. Get it before you submit, because the 30-business-day review clock starts only with a complete application. Certificate of need thresholds were not verified. One secondary source says no CON applies below 25 beds [S36]. Forms are at https://dch.georgia.gov/con-applications-and-forms.
4. **Assemble the application.** The application packet requires:
   - an accurate floor plan showing windows, doors, common areas and resident room measurements, plus digital .jpg photos of the exterior, common areas and a typical resident room [S1];
   - disclosure of every owner holding 10% or more, and the registered agent [S1];
   - a CPA financial stability affidavit, required for ALC and PCH applications of 25 or more beds, stating that the applicant has demonstrated the financial resources to operate (pro forma statements may be used) [S1];
   - a State Fire Marshal inspection report showing no violations and the capacity load, dated within 12 months of submission [S4];
   - an electrical compliance form signed by a Georgia-licensed electrician, dated within 6 months of submission [S4];
   - a water/septic form, and a private water test dated within 6 months if water is not supplied by a city or county system [S4];
   - fingerprint-based GCHEXS background checks for all administrators and individual owners with 10% or more interest, with a determination letter dated within 12 months [S4].
5. **Administrator licensing (25+ beds).** The Board of Long-Term Care Facility Administrators charges $100 plus a processing fee of $10 (paper) or $5 (online), so $110 or $105 (form dated 11.2022) [S6]. Licenses are biennial and expire December 31 of odd-numbered years; renewal fee and CE were not confirmed. The Board is at 237 Coliseum Drive, Macon, GA 31217-3858, phone 404-424-9966. The applicant needs at least one year of full-time practical or managerial experience (at least 1,560 hours in the 12 months before applying) plus a board-approved administration program [S6]. Under 25 beds, no Board license is required, but the administrator needs a satisfactory fingerprint records check before serving [S5][S18][S31].
6. **Pay fees.** The $300 application processing fee is due on submission and is non-refundable. The initial license fee is submitted before the license is issued, and all licensure fees must be paid in full before a permit or license is received [S3][S4]. The annual fee is $350 for a 2–24 bed PCH, $750 for an ALC of 25–50 beds, and $1,500 for 51+ beds. A late fee of $150 applies 60 days past due, a follow-up visit fee is $250, and a permit replacement costs $50 [S3].
7. **DCH review.** DCH states an initial review timeframe of 30 business days from submission, with applications reviewed in the order received. It will not act on an application until it is complete with all attachments and fees [S1][S4]. That 30-business-day figure is from the DCH checklist, not the rule. A 90–120 day estimate in one blog was not confirmed and is not used.
8. **Receive the permit before admitting anyone.** The governing body must hold a valid permit before admitting residents [S1]. Permits are not transferable between communities or locations and cease to be valid when ownership changes [S2]. For PCHs, a change of ownership requires at least 30 days' written notice to DCH and all residents (60 days for impending bankruptcy or eviction) [S18].
9. **After licensure.** Expect unannounced periodic inspections, and keep policies and procedures available for review [S4]. For memory care, the $200 annual certificate is separate. The ALC packet requires a current ALC permit first; PCHs follow the PCH memory care process [S3][S4].

**Medicaid enrollment is separate** (see Section 4). Retroactive enrollment is not permitted, so do not serve a waiver resident expecting payment before enrollment [S21].
